build.gradle.kts<pre><code>dependencies {
    debugImplementation("io.github.otacon.peekniko:client-ktor:&lt;peekniko-version&gt;")
    releaseImplementation("io.github.otacon.peekniko:client-noop:&lt;peekniko-version&gt;")
    implementation("io.ktor:ktor-client-core:&lt;ktor-version&gt;")
}
</code></pre> <p>Keep your Peekniko dependencies on the same version, and do the same for your Ktor dependencies. Keep your app's existing Ktor engine dependency too.</p>

build.gradle.kts<pre><code>dependencies {
    debugImplementation("io.github.otacon.peekniko:client-okhttp:&lt;peekniko-version&gt;")
    releaseImplementation("io.github.otacon.peekniko:client-noop:&lt;peekniko-version&gt;")
    implementation("com.squareup.okhttp3:okhttp:&lt;okhttp-version&gt;")
}
</code></pre> <p>Keep your Peekniko dependencies on the same version and use your app's OkHttp version. If you use custom build variants, choose which ones should capture traffic.</p>

build.gradle.kts<pre><code>dependencies {
    // Debug build
    implementation("io.github.otacon.peekniko:client-ktor:&lt;peekniko-version&gt;")
    implementation("io.ktor:ktor-client-core:&lt;ktor-version&gt;")
    implementation("io.ktor:ktor-client-okhttp:&lt;ktor-version&gt;")
}
</code></pre> <p>Keep your Peekniko dependencies on the same version, and do the same for your Ktor dependencies. This example uses OkHttp, but you can use any supported JVM Ktor engine.</p>

